## Technical FAQs

**Q: Why an IC50 threshold of 1000 nM to define "active"?**

A: IC50 < 1000 nM (pIC50 > 6) is a widely used cutoff in kinase and general QSAR benchmarks. It is a convention, not a biological boundary:
molecules near 1000 nM are close to a coin flip in label terms, and 1,046 structures have records on both sides of the threshold. 
In this dataset 66.7% of molecules are active.

**Q: What does "inactive" mean here?**

A: An IC50 of 1000 nM or higher, either measured exactly or established by a bound such as "IC50 > 10,000 nM". 
These are molecules that were synthesized and tested, often analogs from optimization series, not "does not bind". 
A real screening library contains mostly untested molecules that look different, so this dataset is not representative of a typical screening library.

**Q: How did you handle multiple measurements and duplicate structures?**

A: Structures were standardized and merged by the connectivity block of the InChIKey, so salts, charge states and stereoisomers collapse into one row 
(13,301 ChEMBL IDs became 12,887 structures). Each structure is labelled by the majority of its records, and 463 exact ties were dropped. 
Values for the same compound can differ by orders of magnitude across assays (for example 41, 300 and 7,820 nM).
Assay type and EGFR mutant status are not modelled, and the target ID CHEMBL203 can include measurements on mutant forms. This is a known limitation.

**Q: Did you standardize the molecules before computing features?**

A: Yes. Structures were cleaned with RDKit (functional-group normalization), reduced to the parent fragment (removing salts and solvents) and neutralized. 
Entries without any carbon atom (one bare zinc ion) were removed, while very small organic molecules such as catechol were kept.
In total 20 records failed standardization or were non-organic and were dropped. 
Not applied: tautomer canonicalization, protonation at a given pH, and stereochemistry-aware deduplication 
(stereochemistry is ignored, consistent with the Morgan fingerprints used here).

**Q: How did you handle multiple measurements for the same molecule?**

A: IC50 values from different assays can differ by orders of magnitude
(for example 41, 300 and 7,820 nM for the same compound). I used the
median per molecule, which is robust to single outlier assays, and
removed values below 0.01 nM or above 1,000,000 nM as implausible.
Assay-level differences (cell-based vs enzymatic, wild-type vs mutant
EGFR) were not modelled, and the target ID CHEMBL203 can include
measurements on mutant forms. This is a known limitation.

**Q: Why Morgan fingerprints with radius 2 and 2048 bits?**

A: Morgan fingerprints (ECFP-like) are a standard, strong baseline for
activity prediction. Radius 2 captures substructures up to 2 bonds
around each atom, and 2048 bits is a common size that balances
information and dimensionality. I did not tune these values, so other
settings (for example radius 3, counts instead of bits) could perform
differently.

**Q: Are there any information losses in a 2048-bit fingerprint?**

A: Yes. Different substructures can hash to the same bit (collisions),
and the fingerprint ignores 3D shape and stereochemistry details. This
is acceptable for a baseline, and it is one reason a final docking step
is planned: it adds structural information the fingerprint lacks.

**Q: Did you standardize the molecules before computing features?**

A: SMILES were used as provided by ChEMBL (all 13,518 parsed without
errors in RDKit). I did not apply extra standardization such as salt
stripping, neutralization or tautomer canonicalization, so a small
fraction of molecules may be represented in non-parent forms. Adding a
standardization step is listed as an improvement.

**Q: Why these six descriptors?**

A: They are the Lipinski-related properties (molecular weight, LogP,
H-bond donors and acceptors, TPSA, rotatable bonds), chosen because
they are interpretable and relevant to drug-likeness. Active compounds
showed modestly higher molecular weight, LogP, acceptor count and
rotatable bonds, but no single descriptor separates the classes.


**Q: Why Random Forest and XGBoost?**

A: Both are strong, well-established baselines on fingerprint data and
require little tuning. They also allow a clear comparison between
bagging and boosting. Graph neural networks are a reasonable next step
at this dataset size (about 13.5k molecules), and were left as future
work to keep the scope manageable. Fingerprint plus tree-model baselines
are often hard to beat on datasets of this size.

**Q: Did you tune hyperparameters?**

A: No. Both models use reasonable fixed settings (500 trees, XGBoost
max_depth 6 and learning rate 0.1) and class weighting. Tuning with
cross-validation inside the training set would likely give small gains.
The test set was used only for final evaluation.

**Q: Is Random Forest really better than XGBoost?**

A: Random Forest scored higher on both splits (0.945 vs 0.938 random,
0.890 vs 0.868 scaffold), but this comes from a single split and a
single random seed, without confidence intervals. The result is
suggestive, not conclusive. Repeating with several seeds and scaffold
folds would be needed to claim a real difference.

**Q: Why ROC-AUC, and does 0.89 mean the model would find new EGFR inhibitors?**

A: ROC-AUC is threshold-independent and, together with PR-AUC, handles the 2:1 class imbalance. 
It measures how well the model separates measured actives from measured inactives within this dataset. 
It does not measure screening performance on a library of untested molecules, which is why the next notebook reports enrichment on a decoy library.

**Q: Why did you drop the descriptors if they slightly improved AUC?**

A: Adding descriptors to fingerprints changed scaffold-split ROC-AUC from 0.893 to 0.895, a difference within expected variation.
Descriptors alone reached 0.764. I kept fingerprints only for simplicity.
The choice was made after seeing test-set results, but with a 0.002 difference the risk of selection bias is negligible.

**Q: Why did you evaluate on a scaffold split in addition to a random split, and why are the AUCs different?**

A: In a random split, structurally similar molecules from the same chemical series end up in both the training and test sets, 
so the model is partly rewarded for recognizing near-duplicates. A scaffold split places entire scaffold groups in one set only, 
which is closer to screening new compounds. ROC-AUC drops from 0.943 to 0.893 for Random Forest. 
Both results are reported and the scaffold split is treated as the primary estimate.

**Q: Is the scaffold split strict enough?**

A: Only moderately. Murcko scaffolds ignore side chains, so test molecules can still be close analogs of training molecules:
the median nearest-neighbour Tanimoto similarity is 0.73 on the scaffold split (0.82 on the random split).
A stricter evaluation would cluster molecules by fingerprint similarity or split by publication date.
This was not done, and the scaffold-split AUC should be read as optimistic.

**Q: How much do the models add over a simple similarity baseline?**

A: A Tanimoto nearest-neighbour rule with no training reaches ROC-AUC 0.874 on the scaffold split (0.920 on the random split),
against 0.893 and 0.943 for Random Forest. The models add about 0.02 ROC-AUC, so most of the signal is similarity to known EGFR compounds. 
This is expected for a target with many well-explored chemical series.
